    "faq": {
      "type": "theinmag-teacher-faq",
      "settings": {
        "heading": "Ask away",
        "subhead": "The questions homeschool families actually&nbsp;ask&nbsp;us.",
        "compact": true,
        "show_character": true,
        "bg_image_filename": "background_homeschoolFAQ1.jpg",
        "bg_image_opacity": 30
      },
      "blocks": {
        "q1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Is theINmag a curriculum?",
            "answer": "<p>No. theINmag is a 120+ page print and digital magazine made entirely by Australian kids. It sits alongside whatever you're doing - it's not a replacement for your homeschool curriculum, but it is something your kid will actually want to pick up.</p><p>Most homeschool families use it as a launchpad for rich open tasks, mentor texts, and art prompts.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "How does theINmag fit into a homeschool week?",
            "answer": "<p>However you want. Some families read it cover-to-cover over a weekend. Some pull one spread per day and let it spark a week's worth of learning. Some leave it on the kitchen table and let their kid find it.</p><p>There's no 'right' way. The magazine is designed to be open-ended - your kid drives, you steer when needed.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What ages is theINmag good for?",
            "answer": "<p>Five to thirteen, roughly. Because every page is made by kids of different ages, there's something in every issue for every reader in that range.</p><p>Multi-age families love it - older kids and younger kids can read the same issue and get different things out of it. Younger kids look at the pictures and have things read to them. Older kids dig into the writing, the comics, the science.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q4": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Can I use theINmag as part of my homeschool registration or learning evidence?",
            "answer": "<p>Plenty of families do. Reading theINmag, responding to the prompts, or sending in your kid's own creation all count as legitimate learning - and you'll have a real physical magazine on your shelf as evidence of the work. If your kid gets published in an issue, that's a documented piece of nationally-distributed writing or art for your portfolio.</p><p>Reporting requirements vary by state, so check what your regulator accepts - but the magazine itself gives you real material to work with.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q5": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "My kid doesn't love reading or writing - will they actually engage with this?",
            "answer": "<p>Honestly, this is where theINmag does its best work. The reluctant reader who sees other kids their age in print suddenly wants to read it. The kid who hates writing sees a comic by a 9-year-old and asks if they can make one too.</p><p>Nothing about theINmag feels like school - it's a magazine, made by kids, for kids. That changes everything.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q6": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "What's the difference between the print and digital memberships?",
            "answer": "<p>Both give you the same magazine. Print is the tri-annual paper edition, posted to your letterbox three times a year (February, June, October). Digital is the full PDF version - instantly downloadable, including back issues that have sold out in print.</p><p>Some families pick one. Some get both. There's no wrong answer - see the comparison section above.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q7": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Why don't you have any ads?",
            "answer": "<p>Most kids' magazines make most of their money from advertising. We don't run a single ad. Not one. Kids are already exposed to thousands of ads a week, and we wanted theINmag to be the one place where their attention belongs to them and to the other kids on the page.</p><p>Every issue is funded by memberships and single sales - every dollar goes back into printing, paper, and putting more kids in print.</p>"
          }
        },
        "q8": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "question": "Where can I follow theINmag on Instagram?",
            "answer": "<p>We're at <a href=\"https://www.instagram.com/the.inmag\">@the.inmag</a> on Instagram. That's where we share kid creations, behind-the-scenes from the road, and the day-to-day of running a kid-made magazine out of a van. We'd love to see you over there.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": ["q1", "q2", "q3", "q4", "q5", "q6", "q7", "q8"]
    },
